condition_name: Wax in the Ear
condition_slug: wax-in-the-ear
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: "Wax (or cerumen) is a normal product of the human external ear. A dark brownish mixture of the secretions of the ceruminous and sebaceous glands in the outer third of the external auditory canal. Small quantities are produced continuously and function to lubricate the canal Quantities produced and the consistencies vary May be excessive in some people, causing deafness, ear ache, secondary infection and even vertigo"
clinical_features[1]:
  - type: Clinical Features
    features[6]: Sensation of blockage and some degree of deafness are the most common complaints,"Sometimes, pain and irritation",Ear discharge in some cases,Quantity seen varies,May be soft or hard,May be impacted in the deep meatus
investigations[0]:
treatment:
  goals[1]: Evacuate the wax and clear the ear
  non_drug[3]: "Removal with probe and cotton wool: for soft wax","Ear syringing: for hard wax, often after preliminary softening with oily drops","Occasionally, removal under anaesthesia if syringing is unsuccessful"
  drug[2]: Ear drops to soften and loosen wax,Warm olive oil
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[0]:
  supportive_measures[0]:
differential_diagnoses[2]: Foreign bodies,Otitis externa
complications[2]: "Superimposed infection: otitis externa",Hearing impairment
prevention[0]:
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment: null
prognosis: null